          "caption": "On July 17, 2017, lightning strikes reportedly ignited four wildfires that spread across mountainous areas near Pedrógão Grande, Portugal, creating an inferno that turned deadly. As people fled their homes, a car accident on a small country road trapped dozens of people on the roadway. Flames engulfed the area, resulting in the death of more than forty people at that scene. Before the fires were quenched on July 24, a total of at least 64 people had perished with more than 200 more wounded.\nThe Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) aboard NASA’s Terra satellite captured a true-color image of the fires on June 20. Multiple red hot spots mark areas where the thermal sensor on the instrument detected high temperatures. When accompanied by smoke, as in this image, such hot spots are diagnostic for actively burning fires. Heavy smoke rises from the fires and is blowing to the northwest.\n\nFires across Portugal’s forested landscape during the warm, dry summer months are not uncommon. In 2016, hundreds of fires raged on the mainland and also on the Portuguese island of Madeira. The high death toll associated with this week’s fire, however, led The New York Times and other media to report it as “Portugal’s worst forest fire in more than half a century.”",
          "alt": "On July 17, 2017, lightning strikes reportedly ignited four wildfires that spread across mountainous areas near Pedrógão Grande, Portugal, creating an inferno that turned deadly. As people fled their homes, a car accident on a small country road trapped dozens of people on the roadway. Flames engulfed the area, resulting in the death of more than forty people at that scene. Before the fires were quenched on July 24, a total of at least 64 people had perished with more than 200 more wounded.\nThe Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) aboard NASA’s Terra satellite captured a true-color image of the fires on June 20. Multiple red hot spots mark areas where the thermal sensor on the instrument detected high temperatures. When accompanied by smoke, as in this image, such hot spots are diagnostic for actively burning fires. Heavy smoke rises from the fires and is blowing to the northwest.\n\nFires across Portugal’s forested landscape during the warm, dry summer months are not uncommon. In 2016, hundreds of fires raged on the mainland and also on the Portuguese island of Madeira. The high death toll associated with this week’s fire, however, led The New York Times and other media to report it as “Portugal’s worst forest fire in more than half a century.”",
